      "page": 4,
      "text": "SAME Experiment Status 11-16-2007\nSAME Experiment Motivation/Objectives\nLow-gravity smoke particle sizes are largely unknown but strongly affect detector performance.\nPrior work (Comparative Soot Diagnostics Experiment) demonstrated:\nSmoke detector design must be based on low-g data\nLiquid smoke aerosols require on-orbit measurement of smoke particulate size distribution.\nPower and mass limitations prevent direct measurement of the smoke particle size distribution.\nIntegral measures (moments) of the particle size distribution were chosen as the best approach.\nTarget was to produce data in time to support Orion ECLSS design\n\nGoal:\nImproved reliability of future spacecraft (Orion) smoke detectors by understanding the smoke particulate size distribution.\nObjectives:\nMeasurement three moments of the particle size distribution of μg smoke\nEvaluate the performance in g of the two existing U.S. spacecraft smoke detectors",
